it.2004.0.4.reviewed_statement:
Antonio and Bernardo play the following game. There are two piles of tokens, one with $m$ tokens and the other with $n$ tokens. In turn, each player makes one of the following moves:

• take one token from one of the piles;

• take one token from each of the piles;

• move one token from one pile to the other.

A player who cannot move loses. Antonio starts. Determine, in terms of $m$ and $n$, whether one of the players has a winning strategy, and if so, which one.

it.2004.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Determine whether the following statement is true or false:

For every sequence $x_1, x_2, x_3, \dots$ of non-negative real numbers there exist two sequences $a_1, a_2, a_3, \dots$ and $b_1, b_2, b_3, \dots$ of non-negative real numbers such that

• $x_n = a_n + b_n$ for every $n$;

• $a_1 + \cdots + a_n \le n$ for infinitely many values of $n$;

• $b_1 + \cdots + b_n \le n$ for infinitely many values of $n$ (possibly different from the previous ones).

it.2008.0.3.reviewed_statement:
Find all functions $f : \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying simultaneously the following properties:

• for every pair of integers $(m, n)$ with $m < n$, $f(m) < f(n)$;

• for every pair of integers $(m, n)$ there is an integer $k$ such that $f(m) - f(n) = f(k)$.

it.2008.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $ABC$ be a triangle with all angles greater than $45^\circ$ and less than $90^\circ$.

(a) Prove that three squares with the following properties can be placed inside $ABC$:

• the three squares all have the same side;

• the three squares have a common vertex $K$ inside the triangle;

• any two of the squares have no common point other than $K$;

• each square has two opposite vertices on the perimeter of $ABC$ and otherwise lies entirely inside triangle $ABC$.

(b) Let $P$ be the center of the square with side $AB$ constructed outside $ABC$. Let $r_C$ be the reflection of the line $CK$ in the bisector of angle $\angle ACB$. Prove that $P$ lies on $r_C$.

it.2009.0.3.reviewed_statement:
A positive integer $n$ is called pleasant if it has the following properties:

• its decimal representation consists of $4$ digits;

• the first and the third digits of $n$ are equal;

• the second and the fourth digits of $n$ are equal;

• the product of the digits of $n$ divides $n^2$.

Find all pleasant numbers.

it.2010.0.2.reviewed_statement:
Every non-negative integer (zero included) is coloured white or red so that:

• there is at least one white number and at least one red number;

• the sum of a white number and a red number is white;

• the product of a white number and a red number is red.

Prove that the product of two red numbers is always red and that the sum of two red numbers is always red.

it.2010.0.5.reviewed_statement:
In the land of Cockaigne the following solitaire game is played. One starts from a finite string of zeros and ones, and the following moves are allowed:

(i) delete two consecutive ones;

(ii) delete three consecutive zeros;

(iii) if the string contains the substring $01$, replace this substring by $100$.

The moves are made one at a time, in succession. The game is won if the string is reduced to one of two digits or fewer. (For example, starting from $0101$ one can win by first applying move (iii) to the last two digits, getting $01100$, then move (i) to its two ones, and finally move (ii) to the three remaining zeros, getting the empty string.) Among all $1024$ possible strings of ten digits, how many are there from which it is not possible to win?

it.2011.0.6.reviewed_statement:
Let $X = \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8\}$. We want to colour all $3$-element subsets of $X$ with $k$ colours so that any two disjoint subsets have different colours. Prove that:

(a) $4$ colours are sufficient;

(b) $3$ colours are not sufficient.

it.2013.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $ABC$ be an isosceles triangle with $AB = AC$ and $\angle BAC < 60^\circ$. Let $D$ be the point on $AC$ such that $\angle DBC = \angle BAC$, let $E$ be the intersection of the perpendicular bisector of $BD$ with the line through $A$ parallel to $BC$, and let $F$ be the point on the line $AC$, on the opposite side of $A$ from $C$, such that $FA$ is twice as long as $AC$. Finally, let $r$ be the perpendicular to $AB$ through $F$, $s$ the perpendicular to $AC$ through $E$, and $t$ the line $BD$. Prove that:

(a) the lines $EB$ and $AC$ are parallel;

(b) the lines $r$, $s$ and $t$ are concurrent.

it.2014.0.2.reviewed_statement:
Let $ABC$ be a triangle such that, if $H$ is the foot of the altitude from $C$, then $AH = 3 \cdot HB$. Moreover, let:

• $M$ be the midpoint of $AB$;

• $N$ be the midpoint of $AC$;

• $P$ be the point on the opposite side of line $AC$ from $B$ such that $NP = NC$ and $PC = CB$.

Prove that $\angle APM = \angle PBA$.

it.2020.0.2.reviewed_statement:
Find all pairs $(a, b)$ of positive integers satisfying the following three conditions:

• $b > a$ and $b - a$ is a prime;

• the units digit of $a + b$ is $3$;

• $ab$ is the square of an integer.

it.2020.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $S$ be the set of integers greater than or equal to $2$. A function $f : S \to S$ is called primordial if it has the following properties:

• it is surjective (i.e. for every $s \in S$ there is at least one $n \in S$ with $f(n) = s$);

• it is increasing on primes (i.e. if $p_1 < p_2$ are primes, then $f(p_1) < f(p_2)$);

• for every $n \in S$, $f(n)$ is the product of $f(p)$ over all primes $p$ dividing $n$ (so, for example, $f(360) = f(2^3 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 5) = f(2) \cdot f(3) \cdot f(5)$).

Determine the largest and the smallest possible value of $f(2020)$ as $f$ ranges over all primordial functions.

it.2021.0.1.reviewed_statement:
A positive integer $m$ is called a zero-carrier if there is a positive integer $k$ such that:

• $k$ is a perfect square;

• $k$ is a multiple of $m$;

• the decimal representation of $k$ contains at least $2021$ digits $0$, but its last digit (the rightmost one) is different from $0$.

Find all zero-carriers.

it.2021.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $ABC$ be an acute triangle, let $M$ be the midpoint of $BC$, and let $H$ be the foot of the altitude from $B$. Let $Q$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ABM$, and let $X$ be the intersection of the altitude $BH$ with the perpendicular bisector of $BC$. Prove that the following two facts are equivalent:

(i) the circumcircle of triangle $ACM$, the circumcircle of triangle $AXH$ and the line $CQ$ pass through a common point;

(ii) the lines $BQ$ and $CQ$ are perpendicular.

it.2022.0.1.reviewed_statement:
Determine for which positive integers $n$ there is a positive integer $A$ such that:

• $A$ is a multiple of $2022$;

• the decimal representation of $A$ contains only the digits $0$ and $7$;

• the decimal representation of $A$ contains the digit $7$ exactly $n$ times.

it.2022.0.2.reviewed_statement:
Let $ABC$ be an acute triangle with $AB < AC$. Let:

• $D$ be the foot of the bisector of the angle at $A$;

• $E$ be the point of segment $BC$ (different from $B$) such that $AB = AE$;

• $F$ be the point of segment $BC$ (different from $B$) such that $BD = DF$;

• $G$ be the point of segment $AC$ such that $AB = AG$.

Prove that the circumcircle of triangle $EFG$ is tangent to the line $AC$.

az.2021.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Determine all functions $f$ defined on the set of all positive integers and taking non-negative integer values, satisfying the three conditions:

- (i) $f(n) \neq 0$ for at least one $n$;
- (ii) $f(xy) = f(x) + f(y)$ for every positive integers $x$ and $y$;
- (iii) there are infinitely many positive integers $n$ such that $f(k) = f(n - k)$ for all $k < n$.

az.2024.2.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $m$ and $n$ be positive integers greater than $1$. In each unit square of an $m \times n$ grid lies a coin with its tail side up. A move consists of the following steps:

- select a $2 \times 2$ square in the grid;
- flip the coins in the top-left and bottom-right unit squares;
- flip the coin in either the top-right or bottom-left unit square.

Determine all pairs $(m, n)$ for which it is possible that every coin shows head-side up after a finite number of moves.

kr.2013.0.3.reviewed_statement:
Prove that there exists a monic polynomial $f(x)$ of degree $6$ with integer coefficients satisfying both conditions:

(i) $f(m) \ne 0$ for every integer $m$;

(ii) for every odd positive integer $n$, there is a positive integer $k$ such that $f(k)$ is a multiple of $n$.

kr.2013.0.8.reviewed_statement:
Let $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ be positive integers, and let $X$ be a set of $a + b + c + d$ points in the plane, no three of which are collinear. Prove that there exist two lines $\ell_1$, $\ell_2$ satisfying all of the following:

(i) $\ell_1$ and $\ell_2$ are not parallel;

(ii) neither $\ell_1$ nor $\ell_2$ passes through any point of $X$;

(iii) the four regions into which $\ell_1$ and $\ell_2$ divide the plane contain $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ points of $X$, respectively.

kr.2014.0.6.reviewed_statement:
Find the number of bijections $f : \{1, 2, \dots, 9\} \to \{1, 2, \dots, 9\}$ satisfying both conditions:

(i) $f(1) > f(2)$ and $f(9) < 9$;

(ii) for $i = 3, 4, \dots, 8$: if $f(1), f(2), \dots, f(i-1)$ are all less than $f(i)$, then $f(i+1)$ is also less than $f(i)$.

kr.2014.0.8.reviewed_statement:
Prove that there exists a function $f : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ (where $\mathbb{N}$ is the set of positive integers) satisfying both conditions:

(i) the set $\{f(n) : n \in \mathbb{N}\}$ is finite;

(ii) if nonzero integers $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_{1000}$ satisfy $f(|x_1|) = f(|x_2|) = \cdots = f(|x_{1000}|)$, then $$x_1 + 2x_2 + 2^2x_3 + 2^3x_4 + \cdots + 2^{999}x_{1000} \ne 0.$$

kr.2015.0.4.reviewed_statement:
For positive integers $n$, $k$, $\ell$, let $Q(n, k, \ell)$ be the number of ordered $\ell$-tuples $(a_1, a_2, \dots, a_\ell)$ of positive integers satisfying all four conditions:

(i) $n = a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_\ell$;

(ii) $a_1 > a_2 > \cdots > a_\ell > 0$;

(iii) $a_\ell$ is odd;

(iv) exactly $k$ of the $a_i$ are odd.

For example, since $9 = 8 + 1 = 6 + 3 = 6 + 2 + 1$, we have $Q(9,1,1) = 1$, $Q(9,1,2) = 2$, $Q(9,1,3) = 1$. Prove that if $n > k^2$, then $\displaystyle\sum_{\ell=1}^{n} Q(n, k, \ell)$ is either $0$ or even.

kr.2017.0.1.reviewed_statement:
Let $U$ be the set of the $20$ diagonals of a regular octagon $P_1P_2P_3P_4P_5P_6P_7P_8$. Find the number of sets $S$ satisfying both conditions:

(i) $S$ is a subset of $U$;

(ii) if $P_iP_j \in S$ and $P_jP_k \in S$ with $i \ne k$, then $P_iP_k \in S$.

kr.2018.0.2.reviewed_statement:
For a positive integer $n$, let $p(n)$ be the number of ordered quadruples $(x, y, z, w)$ of nonnegative integers with $x + y + 2z + 3w = n - 1$, and let $q(n)$ be the number of ordered quadruples $(a, b, c, d)$ of nonnegative integers satisfying

(i) $a + b + c + d = n$;

(ii) $a \ge b$, $c \ge d$, and $a \ge d$;

(iii) $b < c$.

Prove that $p(n) = q(n)$ for all $n$.

kr.2021.0.2.reviewed_statement:
For positive integers $n$, $k$, $r$, let $A(n, k, r)$ be the number of ordered $k$-tuples $(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_k)$ of integers satisfying

(i) $x_1 \ge x_2 \ge \cdots \ge x_k \ge 0$;

(ii) $x_1 + x_2 + \cdots + x_k = n$;

(iii) $x_1 - x_k \le r$.

Prove that $A(m, s, t) = A(m, t, s)$ for all positive integers $m$, $s$, $t$.

kr.2021.0.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $n$ be a positive integer. Two countries $A$ and $B$ each have $n$ airports, and $n^2 - 2n + 2$ airlines operate between the two countries. Between each airport of $A$ and each airport of $B$ exactly one airline operates, in both directions, and there are no flights between two airports of the same country. For two distinct airports $P$ and $Q$, a sequence of airports $T_0, T_1, \dots, T_s$ is called a $(P, Q)$-itinerary if

(i) $T_0 = P$ and $T_s = Q$;

(ii) $T_0, T_1, \dots, T_s$ are all distinct;

(iii) there is one airline that operates between $T_0$ and $T_1$, between $T_1$ and $T_2$, $\dots$, and between $T_{s-1}$ and $T_s$.

Assuming every airline operates at least one route, prove that there exist two airports $P$, $Q$ for which there is either no $(P, Q)$-itinerary or exactly one.

kr.2022.0.6.reviewed_statement:
There are $n \ge 4$ islands connected by bridges so that

(1) each bridge connects exactly two islands and passes through no other island;

(2) any two distinct islands are connected by at most one bridge;

(3) there is no sequence of distinct islands $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_{2k}$ ($k \ge 2$) such that $A_i$ and $A_{i+1}$ are connected by a bridge for each $i = 1, 2, \dots, 2k$ (where $A_{2k+1} = A_1$).

Prove that the total number of bridges is at most $\dfrac{3(n-1)}{2}$.

kr.2023.0.3.reviewed_statement:
For a given positive integer $n \ge 2$, find the largest positive integer $A$ for which there exists a polynomial $P(x)$ of degree $n$ with integer coefficients satisfying both conditions:

(1) $P(1), P(2), \dots, P(A)$ are all multiples of $A$;

(2) $P(0) = 0$ and the coefficient of $x$ in $P(x)$ is $1$, that is, $P(x) = c_nx^n + c_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \cdots + c_2x^2 + x$ with $c_n \ne 0$.

kr.2023.0.8.reviewed_statement:
A positive integer $n$ is called special if $n$ is the product of two distinct primes and $n \equiv 2 \pmod 3$. For example, the special numbers not exceeding $50$ are $14$, $26$, $35$, $38$. Prove that for every finite set $S$ of special numbers there exist disjoint sets $A$ and $B$ satisfying all three conditions:

(1) $A \cup B = S$;

(2) $|n(A) - n(B)| \le 1$, where $n(X)$ is the number of elements of $X$;

(3) for every prime $p$, the number of multiples of $p$ in $A$ and the number of multiples of $p$ in $B$ differ by at most $1$.

kf.2013.1.2.reviewed_statement:
Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying both conditions:

(i) $f(x) \ge 0$ for all real $x$;

(ii) for all real numbers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ with $ab + bc + cd = 0$, $$f(a-b) + f(c-d) = f(a) + f(b+c) + f(d).$$

kf.2015.1.3.reviewed_statement:
A city has at least $3$ subway stations, and trains run in both directions. Suppose there is a route in this city that passes through at least $L + 1$ stations without visiting any station twice. Prove that at least one of the following holds:

(i) there exist three distinct stations $A$, $B$, $C$ such that there is no route from $A$ to $B$ avoiding $C$;

(ii) there is a way to start at some station and return to it without visiting any station twice that passes through at least $\lceil\sqrt{2L}\,\rceil$ stations, where $\lceil x \rceil$ is the smallest integer not less than $x$.

kf.2016.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Let $U$ be a set of $m$ triangles. Prove that there always exists a subset $W$ of $U$ satisfying both conditions:

(i) $W$ contains at least $0.45\,m^{4/5}$ triangles;

(ii) there are no six distinct points $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, $F$ such that the six triangles $ABC$, $BCD$, $CDE$, $DEF$, $EFA$, $FAB$ all belong to $W$.

kf.2017.2.6.reviewed_statement:
In a room, $2017$ boxes are arranged in a circle. A set of boxes is called harmonious if it contains at least $2$ boxes and, moving clockwise from each box of the set, the number of boxes passed before first reaching the next box of the set is $0$ or odd. $30$ students enter the room one after another; each chooses several boxes forming a harmonious set and puts a slip with his name into each chosen box. Suppose the set of all boxes containing $30$ slips is not harmonious. Prove that there exist students $A$, $B$ and boxes $a$, $b$ with both properties:

(i) $A$ chose $a$ but not $b$, and $B$ chose $b$ but not $a$;

(ii) the number of boxes other than $a$, $b$ passed when moving clockwise from $a$ to $b$ is not odd, and none of these boxes was chosen by $A$ or $B$.

kf.2020.1.2.reviewed_statement:
There are $2020$ groups, each consisting of one boy and one girl, and every student belongs to exactly one group. Students shake hands in pairs so that all four conditions hold:

(i) no two students of the same sex shake hands;

(ii) the boy and the girl of the same group shake hands exactly once;

(iii) a boy and a girl from different groups shake hands at most once;

(iv) among the $4$ students of any two groups, the total number of handshakes is at least $3$.

Prove that one can choose $4038$ students and arrange them in a circle so that any two neighbors have shaken hands.

kf.2021.0.2.reviewed_statement:
Let $k \ge 8$ be a positive integer. Prove that if there exist positive integers $x$, $y$ satisfying the conditions below, then there are infinitely many such ordered pairs $(x, y)$:

(1) $\dfrac{x^2-2}{y}$ and $\dfrac{y^2-3}{x}$ are positive integers;

(2) $\gcd\left(3x + \dfrac{2(y^2-3)}{x},\ 2y + \dfrac{3(x^2-2)}{y}\right) = k$.

kf.2021.0.4.reviewed_statement:
A high school has $n \ge 2$ clubs $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_n$. Prove that there exist $n - 1$ groups $B_1, B_2, \dots, B_{n-1}$ such that

(1) $A_1 \cup A_2 \cup \cdots \cup A_n = B_1 \cup B_2 \cup \cdots \cup B_{n-1}$;

(2) for $1 \le i < j \le n-1$, $B_i \cap B_j = \emptyset$ and $-1 \le |B_i| - |B_j| \le 1$, where $|X|$ is the number of elements of $X$;

(3) for each $1 \le i \le n-1$ there exist $1 \le k \le l \le n$ with $B_i \subseteq A_k \cup A_l$.

kf.2022.0.6.reviewed_statement:
A set $X$ is called good if it satisfies all three conditions:

(i) $X$ has $2022$ elements;

(ii) each element of $X$ is a closed interval contained in $[0, 1]$;

(iii) for every real number $r$ with $0 \le r \le 1$, at most $1011$ elements of $X$ contain $r$.

For two good sets $A$ and $B$, let $n(A, B)$ be the number of ordered pairs $(I, J)$ with $I \in A$, $J \in B$ and $I \cap J \ne \emptyset$. Find the largest possible value of $n(A, B)$.

kf.2025.2.5.reviewed_statement:
For a subset $T$ of $S = \{1, 2, \dots, 1000\}$, define $\widetilde{T} = \{1001 - t \mid t \in T\}$ (with $\widetilde{T} = \emptyset$ if $T$ is empty). Find the maximum number of elements of a family $\mathcal{P}$ satisfying all three conditions:

- every element of $\mathcal{P}$ is a subset of $S$;

- for any two elements $A$, $B$ of $\mathcal{P}$, $A \cap B$ is nonempty;

- for every element $A$ of $\mathcal{P}$, $\widetilde{A} \in \mathcal{P}$.

kf.2026.2.4.reviewed_statement:
Prove that there is no quadruple $(p, q, r, n)$ of positive integers satisfying both of the following conditions:
- $p$, $q$, $r$ are primes;
- $p^n + q^n + r^n = 2026(p + q)(q + r)(r + p)$.

id.2012.2.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $m$ and $n$ be positive integers, and let $P$ and $Q$ be two $m \times n$ arrays of $0$s and $1$s arranged in $m$ rows and $n$ columns. For example, for $m = 3$ and $n = 4$ one such array is $$\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}.$$ Suppose the two arrays have the following four properties:

- in every row of $P$, the numbers never increase from left to right;

- in every column of $P$, the numbers never increase from top to bottom;

- the sum of the numbers in each row of $P$ equals the sum of the numbers in the same row of $Q$;

- the sum of the numbers in each column of $P$ equals the sum of the numbers in the same column of $Q$.

Prove that the number in row $i$, column $j$ of $P$ equals the number in row $i$, column $j$ of $Q$ for all $i = 1, \dots, m$ and $j = 1, \dots, n$.

id.2021.2.8.reviewed_statement:
On a large $100 \times 100$ chessboard, small boards of size $1 \times 3$ and $3 \times 1$ are to be placed so that:

(i) every square of the chessboard is covered by at most one small board;

(ii) the small boards together cover every square of the large board except one;

(iii) the sides of the small boards are parallel to the sides of the squares of the large board.

Suppose that this requires $H$ boards of size $1 \times 3$ and $V$ boards of size $3 \times 1$. Find all possible pairs $(H, V)$.

id.2023.1.4.reviewed_statement:
Determine whether there exists a positive integer $N$ satisfying all of the following:

(a) $N$ is divisible by $2^{2023}$ but not by $2^{2024}$;

(b) $N$ has only three distinct digits, none of which is zero;

(c) exactly $99.9\%$ of the digits of $N$ are odd.

jp.1992.0.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $A$ be an $m \times n$ matrix $(m \ne n)$ with entries $0$ and $1$. Suppose that for every injective map $f : \{1, 2, \dots, m\} \to \{1, 2, \dots, n\}$ there exists $i$ with $1 \le i \le m$ such that the $(i, f(i))$ entry of $A$ is $0$. Prove that there exist $S \subseteq \{1, 2, \dots, m\}$ and $T \subseteq \{1, 2, \dots, n\}$ such that:

(i) the $(i, j)$ entry is $0$ for all $i \in S$, $j \in T$;

(ii) $|S| + |T| > n$.

jp.1995.0.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $ABCDE$ be a convex pentagon. Let $S$ and $R$ be the intersections of $BE$ with $AC$ and $AD$ respectively, let $T$ and $P$ be the intersections of $BD$ with $CA$ and $CE$ respectively, and let $Q$ be the intersection of $CE$ and $AD$. The triangles $ASR$, $BTS$, $CPT$, $DQP$, $ERQ$ all have area $1$. Find the areas of

(1) the pentagon $PQRST$;

(2) the pentagon $ABCDE$.

jp.2000.0.4.reviewed_statement:
Given an integer $n \ge 3$, prove that there exists a set $A_n$ with the following two properties:

(1) $A_n$ consists of $n$ distinct positive integers;

(2) for every $a \in A_n$, the product of all the other elements of $A_n$ leaves remainder $1$ upon division by $a$.

jp.2003.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Find the largest integer $n$ for which one can place $n$ points in the plane, no three collinear, and colour each of them red, green or yellow so that:

(i) inside every triangle with all vertices red there is a green point;

(ii) inside every triangle with all vertices green there is a yellow point;

(iii) inside every triangle with all vertices yellow there is a red point.

jp.2018.0.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $n$ be an odd positive integer. Prove that it is impossible to write exactly one of the numbers $1$, $2$, $3$ in each cell of a grid extending infinitely in all directions so that all of the following conditions hold:

