a) Compute $|S_3(5)|$.
b) Prove that $|S_n(d)| > 0$ if and only if $d \leq 2n - 1$.

vn.2019.1.3.reviewed_statement:
For a real-coefficient polynomial $f(x) = a_0 + a_1 x + \cdots + a_n x^n$, let $\Gamma(f(x)) = a_0^2 + a_1^2 + \cdots + a_m^2$. Given $P(x) = (x+1)(x+2)\cdots(x+2020)$, prove that there exist at least $2019$ pairwise distinct polynomials $Q_k(x)$ with $1 \leq k \leq 2^{2019}$, each satisfying:
i) $\deg Q_k(x) = 2020$;
ii) $\Gamma(Q_k(x)^n) = \Gamma(P(x)^n)$ for all positive integers $n$.

vn.2022.1.4.reviewed_statement:
For every pair of positive integers $(n, m)$ with $n < m$, let $s(n, m)$ denote the number of positive integers in $[n, m]$ that are coprime with $m$. Find all positive integers $m \geq 2$ satisfying:
i) $\dfrac{s(n,m)}{m-n} \geq \dfrac{s(1,m)}{m}$ for all $n = 1, 2, \ldots, m-1$;
ii) $2022m + 1$ is divisible by $m^2$.

tr.2008.2.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $\mathbb{N}$ be the set of nonnegative integers and $\mathbb{Z}$ the set of all integers. A function $f : \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Z}$ satisfies

(i) $f(0, 0) = 1$, $f(0, 1) = 1$;

(ii) $f(0, k) = 0$ for every $k \notin \{0, 1\}$;

(iii) $f(n, k) = f(n-1, k) + f(n-1, k-2n)$ for all $n \ge 1$ and all $k$.

Find the value of the sum
$$\sum_{k=0}^{\binom{2009}{2}} f(2008, k).$$

tr.2024.2.5.reviewed_statement:
Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R}^+ \to \mathbb{R}^+$ such that
$$\left\{\frac{f(x)}{f(y)}\right\} + \left\{\frac{f(y)}{f(z)}\right\} + \left\{\frac{f(z)}{f(x)}\right\} = \left\{\frac{x}{y}\right\} + \left\{\frac{y}{z}\right\} + \left\{\frac{z}{x}\right\}$$
for all $x, y, z \in \mathbb{R}^+$. (For a real number $x$, $\{x\} = x - \lfloor x \rfloor$, where $\lfloor x \rfloor$ is the integer part of $x$; e.g. $\{2.7\} = 0.7$ and $\{4\} = 0$.)

tr.2025.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $a$ and $b$ be given real numbers. Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ such that
$$f\left(x + f^{100}(y) + a\right) = y + f^{2025}(x) + b$$
for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$. (For a positive integer $k$, $f^k(x) = f(f(\cdots f(x)))$ with $f$ applied $k$ times.)

ru.2004.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Prove that there is no finite set containing more than $2N$ ($N > 3$) pairwise non-collinear vectors in the plane with the following two properties:

1) for any $N$ vectors of this set there are $N - 1$ further vectors of this set such that the sum of all $2N - 1$ vectors is zero;

2) for any $N$ vectors of this set there are $N$ further vectors of this set such that the sum of all $2N$ vectors is zero.

se.2010.0.6.reviewed_statement:
Finitely many cells of an infinite square grid are coloured red. Prove that a number of squares with sides along grid lines can be drawn such that:

(1) no cell belongs to more than one square, although an edge may belong to more than one square;

(2) every red cell lies in one of the squares, and in each such square the proportion of red cells is at least $1/5$ and at most $4/5$.

se.2013.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $n\ge2$ be an integer. Prove that exactly $2^{n-3}n(n-1)$ integer $n$-tuples $(a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n)$ satisfy:

(i) $a_1=0$;

(ii) for every $m$ with $2\le m\le n$, there is an index $i_m$ with $1\le i_m<m$ such that $|a_{i_m}-a_m|\le1$;

(iii) the tuple contains exactly $n-1$ distinct values.

itt.2002.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Determine all functions $f:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)$ satisfying both of the following conditions:

(i) $f(x+yf(x))=f(x)f(y)$ for all $x,y>0$;

(ii) there are at most finitely many positive real numbers $x$ such that $f(x)=1$.

be.2008.0.4.reviewed_statement:
Suppose that $X+Y+Z$ and $X^2+Y^2+Z^2$ are integers divisible by $6$. Prove that:

(a) if $X,Y,Z$ are integers, then $X^n+Y^n+Z^n$ is an integer divisible by $6$ for every positive integer $n$;

(b) if $X,Y,Z$ are real numbers and $XYZ$ is an even integer, then $X^3+Y^3+Z^3$ is an integer divisible by $6$;

(c) if $X,Y,Z$ are real numbers and $XYZ$ is an even integer, then $X^n+Y^n+Z^n$ is an integer divisible by $6$ for every positive integer $n$.

gra.2002.0.2.reviewed_statement:
Last summer a student of the National Technical University of Athens studied for a re-sit examination for $37$ days according to the following rules:

(a) every day he studied for at least one hour;

(b) every day he studied for an integer number of hours, not exceeding $12$;

(c) in total he had to study for at most $60$ hours.

Prove that there were some consecutive days during which he studied for exactly $13$ hours in total.

gra.2005.0.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $k$ be a positive integer and suppose that the equation $$x^3+y^3-2y(x^2-xy+y^2)=k^2(x-y)\qquad(1)$$ has a solution $(x_0,y_0)$ with $x_0,y_0$ non-zero integers and $x_0\neq y_0$. Prove that:

(a) equation (1) has finitely many solutions $(x,y)$ with $x,y$ integers and $x\neq y$;

(b) it is possible to find $11$ further distinct solutions $(X,Y)$ of equation (1), with $X,Y$ non-zero integers and $X\neq Y$, where $X,Y$ are functions of $x_0,y_0$.

gra.2009.0.4.reviewed_statement:
The pairwise distinct complex numbers $z_1,z_2,z_3,z_4,z_5,z_6$ have images $A_1,A_2,A_3,A_4,A_5,A_6$ which are consecutive points of the circle with centre $O(0,0)$ and radius $r>0$. Let $w$ be a root of the equation $z^2+z+1=0$, and suppose that $$z_1w^2+z_3w+z_5=0,\qquad z_2w^2+z_4w+z_6=0.$$ Prove that:

(a) the triangle $A_1A_3A_5$ is equilateral;

(b) $|z_1-z_2|+|z_2-z_3|+|z_3-z_4|+|z_4-z_5|+|z_5-z_6|+|z_6-z_1|=3|z_1-z_4|=3|z_2-z_5|=3|z_3-z_6|$.

gra.2016.0.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $ABC$ be an acute isosceles triangle with $AB=AC$, and let $CD$ be one of its altitudes. The circle $C_2(C,CD)$ meets $AC$ at $K$, the extension of $AC$ at $Z$, and the circle $C_1(B,BD)$ at $E$. The line $DZ$ meets the circle $C_1$ again at $M$. Prove that:

(a) $\angle ZDE=45^\circ$;

(b) the points $E$, $M$, $K$ are collinear;

(c) $BM\parallel EC$.

gra.2020.0.3.reviewed_statement:
The integers from $1$ to $2030$ are written in a row on a board in increasing order. A move $K$ consists of choosing two numbers $a,b$ written in consecutive positions and replacing the pair $(a,b)$ by the number $(a-b)^{2020}$. The move $K$ is repeated until only one number remains on the board. Determine whether this number can be

(a) $2020^{2020}$;

(b) $2021^{2020}$.

su.1976.2.6.reviewed_statement:
The vertices of a regular $n$-gon with centre $O$ are labelled $+1$ or $-1$. In one move one may change all the signs at the vertices of a regular $k$-gon $(2\leq k\leq n)$ with the same centre $O$ (a $2$-gon is a segment whose midpoint is $O$). Prove that in cases (a), (b), (c) there is a labelling from which one can never obtain all $+1$:

(a) $n=15$;

(b) $n=30$;

(c) any $n>2$.

(d) Let $K(n)$ be the largest number of labellings no one of which can be obtained from another. Prove, for example, that $K(200)=2^{80}$.

su.1977.2.8.reviewed_statement:
There are $1000$ tickets numbered $000,001,\ldots,999$ and $100$ boxes numbered $00,01,\ldots,99$. A ticket may be put in a box if the box number can be obtained from the ticket number by deleting one digit. Prove that:

(a) all the tickets can be put into $50$ boxes;

(b) $40$ boxes are not enough;

(c) fewer than $50$ boxes are not enough.

(d) For $10000$ four-digit tickets, where two digits may be deleted, prove that $34$ boxes suffice.

(e) What is the least number of boxes needed for $k$-digit tickets?

su.1979.1.2.reviewed_statement:
Some numbers are written in a notebook. One may add to the list the arithmetic mean of several of the numbers already in it, provided it is not already in the list. Starting with the two numbers $0$ and $1$, prove that one can obtain

(a) $\frac{1}{5}$;

(b) every rational number between $0$ and $1$.

su.1979.2.7.reviewed_statement:
Points $A_1,A_2,\ldots,A_n$ lie in this order on a line, and each of the segments $A_1A_2,A_2A_3,\ldots,A_{n-1}A_n$ is shorter than $1$. One must mark $k-1$ of the points $A_2,\ldots,A_{n-1}$ so that the segments into which the marked points (together with $A_1$ and $A_n$) divide $A_1A_n$ differ pairwise in length by less than $1$. Prove that this is possible

(a) for $k=3$;

(b) for every $k<n-1$.

su.1981.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Find all sequences of positive integers $(k_n)$ such that

(a) $k_n\leq n\sqrt{n}$ for all $n$;

(b) $k_n-k_m$ is divisible by $m-n$ for all $m>n$.

su.1982.2.7.reviewed_statement:
Do there exist polynomials $P,Q,R$ in the variables $x,y,z$ such that for all $x,y,z$

(a) $P\,(x-y+z)^3+Q\,(y-z-1)^3+R\,(z-2x+1)^3=1$;

(b) $P\,(x-y+z)^3+Q\,(y-z-1)^3+R\,(z-x+1)^3=1$?

bg.1963.0.3.reviewed_statement:
In a trapezium $ABCD$ a point $M$ is chosen on the leg $AB$. Circles $k_1$ and $k_2$ with centres $O_1$ and $O_2$ are drawn through $M,A,D$ and through $M,B,C$ respectively. Prove that:

(a) the second intersection point $N$ of $k_1$ and $k_2$ lies on the other leg $CD$ or on its extension;

(b) the length of $O_1O_2$ does not depend on the position of $M$ on $AB$;

(c) the triangles $O_1MO_2$ and $DMC$ are similar.

Find a position of $M$ on $AB$ for which $k_1$ and $k_2$ have equal radii.

bg.1963.0.4.reviewed_statement:
In a tetrahedron $ABCD$ three of the faces are right triangles and the fourth is not an obtuse triangle. Prove that:

(a) the fourth face is a right triangle if and only if exactly two of the plane angles at some vertex of the tetrahedron are equal;

(b) the volume of the tetrahedron equals $\frac{1}{6}$ of the product of its two shortest edges and an edge not lying in the same face with them.

bg.1964.0.3.reviewed_statement:
Two intersecting lines $g_1,g_2$ with $\angle(g_1,g_2)\neq 90^\circ$ and a point $P$ in their plane are given. For a point $M$ of the plane let $M_1$ and $M_2$ be its reflections in $g_1$ and $g_2$. Prove that:

(a) the locus of the points $M$ for which $M_1$, $M_2$ and $P$ are collinear is a circle $k$ through the intersection point of $g_1$ and $g_2$;

(b) $P$ is the orthocentre of a triangle inscribed in $k$ two of whose sides lie on $g_1$ and $g_2$.

bg.1964.0.4.reviewed_statement:
Three pairwise skew lines $a_1,b_1,c_1$, not all parallel to one plane, are given. The lines $a_2,b_2,c_2$ meet them as follows: $a_2$ meets $a_1,b_1,c_1$ at $A,C_2,B_1$; $b_2$ meets them at $C_1,B,A_2$; $c_2$ meets them at $B_2,A_1,C$; and $A$ is the midpoint of $B_1C_2$, $B$ is the midpoint of $C_1A_2$, $C$ is the midpoint of $A_1B_2$. Prove that:

(a) $A$ is the midpoint of $B_2C_1$, $B$ is the midpoint of $C_2A_1$ and $C$ is the midpoint of $A_2B_1$;

(b) the triangles $A_1B_1C_1$ and $A_2B_2C_2$ are congruent.

bg.1967.0.2.reviewed_statement:
Prove that:

(a) if $y<\frac{1}{2}$ and $n\geq 3$ is a positive integer, then $(y+1)^n\geq y^n+(1+2y)^{\frac{n}{2}}$;

(b) if $x,y,z$ and $n\geq 3$ are positive integers with $x^2-1\leq 2y$, then $x^n+y^n\neq z^n$.

bg.1970.2.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $\delta_0=A_0B_0C_0$ be a triangle. Squares are constructed on the sides $B_0C_0$, $C_0A_0$, $A_0B_0$ in the half-planes not containing $A_0$, $B_0$, $C_0$ respectively, and $A_1,B_1,C_1$ are their centres. In the same way the triangle $\delta_2=A_2B_2C_2$ is constructed from $\delta_1=A_1B_1C_1$, then $\delta_3$ from $\delta_2$, and so on. Prove that:

(a) the segments $A_0A_1$, $B_0B_1$, $C_0C_1$ are equal and perpendicular to $B_1C_1$, $C_1A_1$, $A_1B_1$ respectively;

(b) the vertices $A_1,B_1,C_1$ lie on the segments $A_0A_3$, $B_0B_3$, $C_0C_3$ respectively and divide them in the ratio $2:1$.

bg.1970.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Points $A,B,C$ and a sphere with centre $O$ and radius $1$ are given in space. Find the points $X$ of the sphere for which $f(X)=XA^2+XB^2+XC^2$ attains its maximum and its minimum. Prove that if $OA,OB,OC$ are pairwise perpendicular and $d$ is the distance from $O$ to the centroid of triangle $ABC$, then

(a) the maximum of $f(X)$ equals $9d^2+3+6d$;

(b) the minimum of $f(X)$ equals $9d^2+3-6d$.

bg.1971.2.6.reviewed_statement:
In a triangular pyramid $SABC$ one of the plane angles at $S$ is right, and the orthogonal projection of $S$ on the plane $ABC$ is the orthocentre of $ABC$. Let $SA=m$, $SB=n$, $SC=p$, let $r$ be the inradius of $ABC$ and $H$ the height of the pyramid, and let $r_1,r_2,r_3$ be the inradii of the sections of the pyramid by the planes through the height and $SA$, $SB$, $SC$ respectively. Prove that

(a) $m^2+n^2+p^2\geq 18r^2$;

(b) $\frac{r_1}{H},\frac{r_2}{H},\frac{r_3}{H}$ lie in the interval $(0.4,\,0.5)$.

bg.1972.2.6.reviewed_statement:
In a tetrahedron $ABCD$ every two opposite edges are perpendicular. Prove that:

(a) the four altitudes of $ABCD$ pass through a common point $H$;

(b) $AH+BH+CH+DH<p+2R$, where $p$ is the sum of the lengths of all edges and $R$ is the circumradius of $ABCD$.

bg.1973.1.1.reviewed_statement:
The sequence $a_1,a_2,\ldots$ is defined by $a_1=2$ and $a_{n+1}=a_n^2-a_n+1$ $(n=1,2,\ldots)$. Prove that:

(a) $a_m$ and $a_n$ are relatively prime for $m\neq n$;

(b) $\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^n\frac{1}{a_k}=1$.

bg.1989.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $p$ be a real number and $f(x)=x^p-x+p$. Prove that:

(a) every root $\alpha$ of $f$ satisfies $|\alpha|<p^{\frac{1}{p-1}}$;

(b) if $p$ is a prime, then $f(x)$ cannot be written as a product of two non-constant polynomials with integer coefficients.

bg.1989.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Let $x,y,z$ be pairwise coprime positive integers and $p\geq 5$ and $q$ primes such that:

(i) $6p$ does not divide $q-1$;

(ii) $q$ divides $x^2+xy+y^2$;

(iii) $q$ does not divide $x+y-z$.

Prove that $x^p+y^p\neq z^p$.

bg.1991.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Prove that for every prime $p\geq 5$:

(a) $p^3$ divides $\binom{2p}{p}-2$;

(b) $p^3$ divides $\binom{kp}{p}-k$ for every positive integer $k$.

bg.1993.2.5.reviewed_statement:
$Oxy$ is a rectangular coordinate system in the plane. To each ordered pair of points $A_1,A_2$ of the plane, different from $O$, with coordinates $(x_1,y_1)$ and $(x_2,y_2)$, a real number $f(A_1,A_2)$ is assigned so that:

(a) if $OA_1=OB_1$, $OA_2=OB_2$ and $A_1A_2=B_1B_2$, then $f(A_1,A_2)=f(B_1,B_2)$;

(b) there is a polynomial of second degree $F(u,v,w,z)$ such that $f(A_1,A_2)=F(x_1,y_1,x_2,y_2)$;

(c) there is a number $\phi\in(0,\pi)$ such that $f(A_1,A_2)=0$ whenever $\angle A_1OA_2=\phi$;

(d) if the triangle $OA_1A_2$ is equilateral with side $1$, then $f(A_1,A_2)=\frac{1}{2}$.

Prove that $f(A_1,A_2)=\overrightarrow{OA_1}\cdot\overrightarrow{OA_2}$ for every ordered pair of points $A_1,A_2$.

bg.1997.1.2.reviewed_statement:
Let $M$ be the centroid of triangle $ABC$. Prove the inequality $$\sin\angle CAM+\sin\angle CBM\leq\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}$$

(a) if the circumcircle of triangle $AMC$ is tangent to the line $AB$;

(b) for any triangle $ABC$.

bg.2001.2.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $n\geq 2$ be an integer. At every point $(i,j)$ with $i,j\in\mathbb{Z}$ the remainder of $i+j$ modulo $n$ is written. Find all pairs $(a,b)$ of positive integers such that the rectangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(a,0)$, $(a,b)$, $(0,b)$ has the following properties:

(i) the remainders $0,1,\ldots,n-1$ written at its interior points appear equally often;

(ii) the remainders $0,1,\ldots,n-1$ written at its boundary points appear equally often.

bg.2004.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Let $p$ be a prime and let $0\leq a_1<a_2<\cdots<a_m<p$ and $0\leq b_1<b_2<\cdots<b_n<p$ be integers. Let $k$ be the number of distinct residues modulo $p$ of the sums $a_i+b_j$ $(1\leq i\leq m,\ 1\leq j\leq n)$. Prove that

(a) if $m+n>p$, then $k=p$;

(b) if $m+n\leq p$, then $k\geq m+n-1$.

bg.2005.1.2.reviewed_statement:
Two circles $k_1$ and $k_2$ touch externally at $T$. A line touches $k_2$ at $X$ and meets $k_1$ at $A$ and $B$. Let $S$ be the second intersection point of $k_1$ with the line $XT$. A point $C$ is chosen on the arc $TS$ not containing $A$ and $B$. Let $CY$ be the tangent to $k_2$ with $Y\in k_2$ such that the segment $CY$ does not meet the segment $ST$, and let $I=XY\cap SC$. Prove that:

(a) the points $C,T,Y,I$ are concyclic;

(b) $I$ is the excentre of triangle $ABC$ opposite $A$ (the centre of the excircle tangent to $BC$).

bg.2011.2.6.reviewed_statement:
$2011$ points are given in the interior of a convex $2011$-gon so that no three of the $4022$ points (the interior points and the vertices) are collinear. The points are coloured in two colours, and a colouring is called good if some of the points can be joined by segments so that:

(1) each segment joins two points of the same colour;

(2) no two segments intersect;

(3) any two points of the same colour are connected by a path of segments.

Find the number of good colourings.

bg.2012.1.2.reviewed_statement:
Prove that the positive integers can be coloured in two colours so that:

(1) for every prime $p$ and every positive integer $n$, the numbers $p^n$, $p^{n+1}$, $p^{n+2}$ do not all have the same colour;

(2) there is no infinite geometric progression of positive integers all of the same colour.

bg.2015.2.4.reviewed_statement:
Find all functions $f:\mathbb{R}^+\to\mathbb{R}^+$ such that for all $x,y\in\mathbb{R}^+$:

(i) $f(x+y)\geq f(x)+y$;

(ii) $f(f(x))\leq x$.

pl.1956.2.4.reviewed_statement:
Prove that if positive integers $a,b,c$ satisfy $a^2+b^2=c^2$, then:

(1) at least one of $a$, $b$ is divisible by $3$;

(2) at least one of $a$, $b$ is divisible by $4$;

(3) at least one of $a$, $b$, $c$ is divisible by $5$.

pl.1968.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Consider a set of $n>3$ points in the plane, no three collinear, and a positive integer $k<n$. Prove that:

(a) if $k\leq\frac{n}{2}$, each point can be joined by segments to at least $k$ other points so that no three segments form a triangle;

(b) if $k>\frac{n}{2}$ and each point is joined by segments to at least $k$ other points, then some three segments form a triangle.

pl.1971.2.6.reviewed_statement:
A regular tetrahedron with edge $1$ is given. The distance between two points of its surface $S$ is the length of the shortest polygonal line on $S$ joining them. Prove that:

(a) there are four points of $S$ such that every point of the surface is at distance at most $\frac{1}{2}$ from one of them;

(b) there are no three points with this property.

pl.1973.1.3.reviewed_statement:
A polyhedron $W$ has the following properties:

(i) it has a centre of symmetry;

(ii) the section of $W$ by any plane through the centre of symmetry and an edge of $W$ is a parallelogram;

(iii) some vertex of $W$ belongs to exactly three edges.

Prove that $W$ is a parallelepiped.

pl.1991.2.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $V$ be the set of all vectors with integer coordinates in the Cartesian plane. Find all functions $f:V\to\mathbb{R}$ such that:

(i) $f(v)=1$ for each of the four vectors $v\in V$ of unit length;

(ii) $f(v+w)=f(v)+f(w)$ for any two perpendicular vectors $v,w\in V$ (the zero vector is perpendicular to every vector).

cz.1955.0.1.reviewed_statement:
$ABCD$ is a trapezoid with $AB\parallel CD$ and $AB>CD$. Let $E=AC\cap BD$ and $F=AD\cap BC$. The line through $E$ parallel to $AB$ meets $AD$ at $G$ and $BC$ at $H$, and $K,L$ are the midpoints of $AB$ and $CD$. Prove that:

(a) $K$ and $L$ lie on the line $EF$;

(b) the lines $AC$ and $KH$, and the lines $BD$ and $KG$, are not parallel (let $M=AC\cap KH$, $N=BD\cap KG$);

(c) the points $F,M,N$ are collinear.

cz.1965.0.4.reviewed_statement:
A hollow cube $ABGCDEPF$ (with square faces $ABGC$, $DEPF$ and $AD\parallel BE\parallel GP\parallel CF$) stands on a table so that its space diagonal $AP=1$ is perpendicular to the table. Water is poured into it, and $x$ denotes the length of the part of $AP$ under water. Express the volume $y$ of the water in terms of $x$ when

(a) $0<x\leq\frac{1}{3}$;

(b) $\frac{1}{3}<x\leq\frac{1}{2}$.

cz.1984.2.6.reviewed_statement:
A function $f:\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z}$ satisfies $f(f(m))=-m$ for all $m\in\mathbb{Z}$. Prove that:

(a) $f$ is a bijection of $\mathbb{Z}$ onto itself;

(b) $f(-m)=-f(m)$ for all $m$;

(c) $f(m)=0$ if and only if $m=0$.

Also give an example of such a function.

cz.1996.1.1.reviewed_statement:
A sequence satisfies $G(0)=0$ and $G(n)=n-G(G(n-1))$ for every $n\in\mathbb{N}$. Prove that:

(a) $G(k)\geq G(k-1)$ for every $k\in\mathbb{N}$;

(b) there is no integer $k$ with $G(k-1)=G(k)=G(k+1)$.

nl.1962.0.4.reviewed_statement:
For a number $g$, $[g]$ denotes the greatest integer less than or equal to $g$ (for example $[3\frac12]=3$, $[\frac34]=0$, $[2]=2$). Let $n$ be a positive integer written in the decimal system. Using familiar mathematical notation together with the notation $[\ \cdot\ ]$, express in terms of $n$:

(a) the last digit of $n$;

(b) the second-to-last digit of $n$ (when $n\ge 10$);

(c) the first digit of $n$.

nl.1964.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Consider a sequence $g_1,g_2,\dots$ of non-negative integers, each written with three digits (numbers below $100$ are also written with three digits; for example $27$ is written $027$). Each number is obtained from the previous one by taking the product of its three digits. The sequence of course depends on the choice of $g_1$ (for example, $g_1=359$ gives $g_2=135$, $g_3=015$, $g_4=000$). Show that, whatever the choice of $g_1$:

(a) $g_{n+1}\le\frac34 g_n$;

(b) $g_{10}=000$.

nl.1965.0.2.reviewed_statement:
Consider ten consecutive integers. Let $S_1$ be the sum of the squares of the first five of them and $S_2$ the sum of the squares of the last five. Prove:

(a) $S_1$ is divisible by $5$;

(b) $S_1$ is not the square of an integer;

(c) the product $S_1\cdot S_2$ is divisible by $150$.

nl.1965.0.5.reviewed_statement:
The function $f$, defined for all real numbers, satisfies $$f(x+y)+f(x-y)=2f(x)+2f(y)$$ for all $x$ and $y$. Prove:

(a) $f(0)=0$;

(b) $f(-x)=f(x)$ for every $x$;

(c) $f(2x)=4f(x)$ for every $x$;

(d) $f(x+y+z)=f(x+y)+f(y+z)+f(z+x)-f(x)-f(y)-f(z)$ for all $x$, $y$, $z$.

nl.1972.0.2.reviewed_statement:
Prove that there is exactly one function $f$ defined for all real numbers such that

(a) if $x\le y$, then $f(x)\le f(y)$;

(b) $f(f(x))=x$ for every $x$.

nl.1981.0.4.reviewed_statement:
A wire figure is held in various positions in a beam of parallel light rays, so that various shadow figures appear in a plane perpendicular to the rays. In this way one can obtain:

(a) an isosceles triangle;

(b) an isosceles triangle with the altitude from the apex;

(c) a rectangle containing an isosceles triangle;

(d) a rhombus with one diagonal.

The wire figure consists of eight straight pieces of iron wire, each piece being attached at both ends to at least one other piece. Find a figure satisfying this description, and give the direction of the light rays producing each of the shadow figures (a) to (d).

nl.1990.0.3.reviewed_statement:
For the function $f(x)=ax^4+bx^3+cx^2+dx$ it is given that:

