ua.1997.0.6.reviewed_statement:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{Q}^{+} \to \mathbb{Q}^{+}$ which satisfy the conditions

(i) $f(x+1)=f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Q}^{+}$;

(ii) $f(x^2)=f(x)^2$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Q}^{+}$.

kr.2008.0.4.reviewed_statement:
We say that sets $A, B, C$ form a partition of $\mathbb{N}$ if (i) $A, B, C \neq \emptyset$; (ii) $A \cap B = B \cap C = C \cap A = \emptyset$; (iii) $A \cup B \cup C = \mathbb{N}$.

Prove that there is no partition $A,B,C$ of $\mathbb{N}$ satisfying the following:

(i) for all $a \in A$, $b \in B$ we have $a+b+2008 \in C$;

(ii) for all $b \in B$, $c \in C$ we have $b+c+2008 \in A$;

(iii) for all $c \in C$, $a \in A$ we have $c+a+2008 \in B$.

kr.2008.0.7.reviewed_statement:
Prove that the only function $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the following conditions is $f(x)=x$:

(i) $f(x) = x^2f\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)$ for all $x \neq 0$;

(ii) $f(x+y) = f(x)+f(y)$ for all $x, y$;

(iii) $f(1)=1$.

kr.1997.0.6.reviewed_statement:
Find all polynomials $P(x,y)$ such that for all reals $x,y$

(i) $x^{100}+y^{100}\le P(x,y)\le 101(x^{100}+y^{100})$;

(ii) $(x-y)P(x,y)=(x-1)P(x,1)+(1-y)P(1,y)$.

kr.1996.0.6.reviewed_statement:
Find the minimum value of $k$ for which there exist two sequences $(a_i)$, $(b_i)$, $i=1,2,\dots ,k$, satisfying the following conditions:

(i) $a_i,b_i$ belong to $S=\{1996^n\mid n=0,1,2,\dots\}$ for all $i$;

(ii) $a_i\ne b_i$ for all $i$;

(iii) $a_i\le a_{i+1}$ and $b_i\le b_{i+1}$ for all $i$;

(iv) $\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_i=\sum_{i=1}^{k} b_i$.

kf.2001.1.1.reviewed_statement:
Given an odd prime $p$, find all functions $f:\mathbb Z \to \mathbb Z$ satisfying the following two conditions:

(i) $f(m)=f(n)$ for all $m,n \in \mathbb Z$ such that $m\equiv n\pmod p$;

(ii) $f(mn)=f(m)f(n)$ for all $m,n \in \mathbb Z$.

kf.1997.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Find all pairs of functions $f, g: \mathbb R \to \mathbb R$ such that

(i) if $x < y$, then $f(x) < f(y)$;

(ii) $f(xy) = g(y)f(x) + f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb R$.

kf.1997.2.5.reviewed_statement:
For positive numbers $a_1,a_2,\dots,a_n$ define $$A=\frac{a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_n}{n}, \quad G=\sqrt[n]{a_1\cdots a_n}, \quad H=\frac{n}{a_1^{-1}+\cdots+a_n^{-1}}.$$ Prove that

(i) $\frac{A}{H}\leq -1+2\left(\frac{A}{G}\right)^n$ for $n$ even;

(ii) $\frac{A}{H}\leq -\frac{n-2}{n}+\frac{2(n-1)}{n}\left(\frac{A}{G}\right)^n$ for $n$ odd.

in.1987.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Find a finite sequence of $16$ numbers such that

(a) it reads the same from left to right as from right to left;

(b) the sum of any $7$ consecutive terms is $-1$;

(c) the sum of any $11$ consecutive terms is $+1$.

itt.1996.1.4.reviewed_statement:
Prove that there exists a set $X$ of $1996$ positive integers with the following properties:

(i) the elements of $X$ are pairwise coprime;

(ii) all elements of $X$ and all sums of two or more distinct elements of $X$ are composite numbers.

itt.1993.1.4.reviewed_statement:
An $m \times n$ chessboard with $m,n \ge 2$ is given. Some dominoes are placed on the chessboard so that the following conditions are satisfied:

(i) each domino occupies two adjacent squares of the chessboard;

(ii) it is not possible to put another domino onto the chessboard without overlapping;

(iii) it is not possible to slide a domino horizontally or vertically without overlapping.

Prove that the number of squares not covered by a domino is less than $\frac15 mn$.

rst.2025.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Find all functions $f : \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Z}$ such that

• $f$ is strictly increasing;

• there exists $M \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $f(x+1) - f(x) < M$ for all $x \in \mathbb{N}$;

• for every $x \in \mathbb{Z}$ there exists $y \in \mathbb{Z}$ such that $$f(y) = \frac{f(x) + f(x + 2024)}{2}.$$

rst.2025.2.6.reviewed_statement:
For an $n \times n$ table filled with natural numbers, we say it is a divisor table if

• the numbers in the $i$-th row are exactly all the divisors of some natural number $r_i$;

• the numbers in the $j$-th column are exactly all the divisors of some natural number $c_j$;

• $r_i \ne r_j$ for every $i \ne j$.

A prime number $p$ is given. Determine the smallest natural number $n$, divisible by $p$, such that there exists an $n \times n$ divisor table, or prove that such $n$ does not exist.

rst.2003.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Each side and each diagonal of a convex $n$-gon $(n\geq 3)$ is colored red or blue. Prove that the vertices of the $n$-gon can be labeled $A_1,A_2,\ldots,A_n$ in such a way that one of the following two conditions is satisfied:

(a) all segments $A_1A_2,A_2A_3,\ldots,A_{n-1}A_n,A_nA_1$ have the same colour;

(b) there exists a number $k$, $1<k<n$, such that the segments $A_1A_2,A_2A_3,\ldots,A_{k-1}A_k$ are blue and the segments $A_kA_{k+1},\ldots,A_{n-1}A_n,A_nA_1$ are red.

rot.1992.3.9.reviewed_statement:
Let $x, y$ be real numbers such that $1\le x^2-xy+y^2\le2$. Show that

(a) $\frac{2}{9}\le x^4+y^4\le 8$;

(b) $x^{2n}+y^{2n}\ge\frac{2}{3^n}$ for all $n\ge3$.

irt.2012.2.5.reviewed_statement:
The function $f:[0,+\infty) \to [0,+\infty)$ satisfies the following properties for all $a,b\geq 0$:

(a) $f(a)=0 \Leftrightarrow a=0$;

(b) $f(ab)=f(a)f(b)$;

(c) $f(a+b)\le 2 \max \{f(a),f(b)\}$.

Prove that $f(a+b)\le f(a)+f(b)$ for all $a,b\geq 0$.

irt.2014.5.14.reviewed_statement:
Is there a function $f:\mathbb{N}\to \mathbb{N}$ such that

(i) there exists $n\in \mathbb{N}$ with $f(n)\neq n$;

(ii) the number of divisors of $m$ is $f(n)$ if and only if the number of divisors of $f(m)$ is $n$?

irt.2016.3.9.reviewed_statement:
Let $n$ be a positive integer. Two players $A$ and $B$ play a game in which they take turns choosing positive integers $k \le n$. The rules of the game are:

(i) a player cannot choose a number that has been chosen by either player on any previous turn;

(ii) a player cannot choose a number consecutive to any of those the player has already chosen on any previous turn;

(iii) the game is a draw if all numbers have been chosen; otherwise the player who cannot choose a number anymore loses the game.

The player $A$ takes the first turn. Determine the outcome of the game, assuming that both players use optimal strategies.

irt.2018.3.7.reviewed_statement:
Find all functions $f:\mathbb{R}\to \mathbb{R}$ that satisfy the following conditions:

(a) $x+f(y+f(x))=y+f(x+f(y))$ for all $x,y \in \mathbb{R}$;

(b) the set $I=\left\{\frac{f(x)-f(y)}{x-y}\mid x,y\in \mathbb{R},\ x\neq y \right\}$ is an interval.

irt.2018.5.15.reviewed_statement:
Let $n>1$, and let distinct positive integers $a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_{n+1}$ be given. Does there exist a polynomial $p(x)\in\mathbb{Z}[x]$ of degree at most $n$ satisfying the following conditions?

(a) $\gcd(p(a_i),p(a_j))>1$ for all $1\le i < j\le n+1$;

(b) $\gcd(p(a_i),p(a_j),p(a_k))=1$ for all $1\le i < j < k\le n+1$.

irt.2020.1.3.reviewed_statement:
We call a number $n$ interesting if for each permutation $\sigma$ of $1,2,\ldots,n$ there exist polynomials $P_1,P_2,\ldots ,P_n$ and $\epsilon > 0$ such that

(i) $P_1(0)=P_2(0)=\cdots =P_n(0)$;

(ii) $P_1(x)>P_2(x)>\cdots >P_n(x)$ for $-\epsilon<x<0$;

(iii) $P_{\sigma (1)} (x)>P_{\sigma (2)}(x)> \cdots >P_{\sigma (n)} (x)$ for $0<x<\epsilon$.

Find all interesting $n$.

irt.2022.0.12.reviewed_statement:
Let $A$ be the set of all closed intervals $[a,b] \subset \mathbb{R}$. Find all functions $f:\mathbb{R} \to A$ such that

• $x \in f(y) \iff y \in f(x)$;

• $|x-y|>2 \iff f(x) \cap f(y)=\varnothing$;

• $f(r)=[r^2-1,r^2+1]$ for all real numbers $0\leq r\leq 1$.

tw.1995.2.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $m_1,m_2,\ldots,m_n$ be pairwise distinct integers. Prove that there exists a polynomial $f(x)\in\mathbb{Z}[x]$ of degree $n$ satisfying the following two conditions:

(a) $f(m_i)=-1$ for all $i=1,2,\ldots,n$;

(b) $f(x)$ is irreducible.

il.2016.0.4.reviewed_statement:
At the beginning there is a circle with three points on it, colored (clockwise) green, blue, red. Jonathan may perform the following actions, as many times as he wants, in any order:

• choose two adjacent points with different colors, and add a point between them having one of these two colors;

• choose two adjacent points with the same color, and add a point between them with any of the three colors;

• choose three adjacent points, at least two of which have the same color, and delete the middle one.

Can Jonathan reach a state in which only three points remain on the circle, colored (clockwise) blue, green, red?

by.1999.2.8.reviewed_statement:
Let $n$ be an integer greater than $2$. A positive integer is called attainable if it is $1$ or can be obtained from $1$ by a sequence of operations with the following properties:

1. the first operation is either an addition or a multiplication;

2. thereafter, additions and multiplications are used alternately;

3. in each addition one may choose independently whether to add $2$ or $n$;

4. in each multiplication one may choose independently whether to multiply by $2$ or by $n$.

A positive integer which cannot be so obtained is called unattainable.

(a) Prove that if $n\geq 9$, there are infinitely many unattainable positive integers.

(b) Prove that if $n=3$, all positive integers except $7$ are attainable.

by.2008.1.4.reviewed_statement:
Find the maximal number of planes for which there exist $6$ points such that

(1) every plane contains at least $4$ of the points;

(2) no line passes through $4$ of the points.

by.2011.1.1.reviewed_statement:
Nonzero real numbers $a,b,c$ with $b>0$ are such that the equation $ax^2+bx-c=0$ has two distinct roots which are also roots of the equation $x^3+bx^2+ax-c=0$. Prove the inequalities

(a) $abc>16$;

(b) $abc\geq\frac{3125}{108}$.

by.2018.2.7.reviewed_statement:
Consider the expression $M(n, m)=|n\sqrt{n^2+a}-bm|$, where $n$ and $m$ are arbitrary positive integers and $a$ and $b$ are fixed numbers: $a$ is an odd positive integer and $b$ is a rational number whose irreducible fraction has an odd denominator. Prove that

(a) there are at most finitely many pairs $(n, m)$ with $M(n, m)=0$;

(b) there is a positive constant $C$ such that $M(n, m)\geqslant C$ for all pairs $(n, m)$ with $M(n, m)\ne 0$.

za.2004.0.6.reviewed_statement:
The numbers $a_1,a_2,a_3$ are distinct positive integers such that

(i) $a_1$ divides $a_2+a_3+a_2a_3$;

(ii) $a_2$ divides $a_3+a_1+a_3a_1$;

(iii) $a_3$ divides $a_1+a_2+a_1a_2$.

Prove that $a_1,a_2,a_3$ cannot all be prime.

za.2006.0.6.reviewed_statement:
Consider the function $f$ defined by $$f(n)=\frac{1}{n}\left(\left\lfloor\frac{n}{1}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor+\cdots+\left\lfloor\frac{n}{n}\right\rfloor \right)$$ for all positive integers $n$. Prove that

(a) $f(n+1)>f(n)$ for infinitely many $n$;

(b) $f(n+1)<f(n)$ for infinitely many $n$.

za.2007.0.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $ABC$ be a triangle and $PQRS$ a square with $P$ on $AB$, $Q$ on $AC$, and $R$ and $S$ on $BC$. Let $H$ be the point on $BC$ such that $AH$ is the altitude from $A$. Prove that

(a) $\frac{1}{AH} +\frac{1}{BC}=\frac{1}{PQ}$;

(b) the area of $ABC$ is twice the area of $PQRS$ if and only if $AH=BC$.

za.2007.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{R}$ be a function satisfying

(i) $f(n) \ge 0$ for all $n \in \mathbb{Z}$;

(ii) $f(mn)=f(m)f(n)$ for all $m,n \in \mathbb{Z}$;

(iii) $f(m+n) \le \max(f(m),f(n))$ for all $m,n \in \mathbb{Z}$.

(a) Prove that $f(n) \le 1$ for all $n \in \mathbb{Z}$.

(b) Find a function $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying (i), (ii), (iii), $0<f(2)<1$ and $f(2007) = 1$.

za.2009.0.5.reviewed_statement:
A game is played on a board with an infinite row of holes labelled $0, 1, 2, \ldots$. Initially $2009$ pebbles are put into hole $1$; the other holes are empty. Steps are performed according to the following rules:

(i) at each step, two pebbles are removed from one of the holes (if possible), and one pebble is put into each of the neighbouring holes;

(ii) no pebbles are ever removed from hole $0$;

(iii) the game ends when no hole with a positive label contains at least two pebbles.

Show that the game always terminates and that the number of pebbles in hole $0$ at the end of the game does not depend on the specific sequence of steps. Determine this number.

za.2009.0.6.reviewed_statement:
Let $A$ denote the set of real numbers $x$ with $0\le x<1$. A function $f:A\to \mathbb{R}$ has the properties

(i) $f(x)=2f\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)$ for all $x\in A$;

(ii) $f(x)=1-f\left(x-\frac{1}{2}\right)$ if $\frac{1}{2}\le x<1$.

Prove that

(a) $f(x)+f(1-x)\ge \frac{2}{3}$ if $x$ is rational and $0<x<1$;

(b) there are infinitely many odd positive integers $q$ such that equality holds in (a) for $x=\frac{1}{q}$.

za.2011.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $\mathbb{N}_0$ denote the set of all nonnegative integers. Determine all functions $f:\mathbb{N}_0\to\mathbb{N}_0$ with the following two properties:

• $0\le f(x)\le x^2$ for all $x\in\mathbb{N}_0$;

• $x-y$ divides $f(x)-f(y)$ for all $x,y\in\mathbb{N}_0$ with $x>y$.

za.2016.0.1.reviewed_statement:
At the start of the first game of the season of the Mighty Mathematicians Football Team, the coach noticed that the jersey numbers of the $22$ players on the field were all the numbers from $1$ to $22$. At halftime, the coach substituted her goalkeeper, with jersey number $1$, by a reserve player. No other substitutions were made by either team at or before halftime. The coach noticed that after the substitution no two players on the field had the same jersey number and that the sums of the jersey numbers of the two teams were exactly equal. Determine

• the greatest possible jersey number of the reserve player;

• the smallest possible (positive) jersey number of the reserve player.

za.2022.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $n \geq 3$ be an integer, and consider a set of $n$ points in three-dimensional space such that

(i) every two distinct points are connected by a string which is red, green, blue or yellow;

(ii) for every three distinct points, if the three strings between them are not all of the same colour, then they are of three different colours;

(iii) not all the strings have the same colour.

Find the maximum possible value of $n$.

za.2025.0.2.reviewed_statement:
A sequence of letters, each of which is $A$, $B$, $C$ or $D$, is called alternating if

• between every two $A$'s there is at least one $B$;

• between every two $B$'s there is at least one $A$;

• between every two $C$'s there is at least one $D$;

• between every two $D$'s there is at least one $C$.

Calculate the number of alternating sequences of length $6$.

za.2026.0.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $n \geqslant 2$ be an integer, and let $r_n$ be the remainder when $n^{n - 1} - 1$ is divided by $(n - 1)^3$. Prove that

(a) for each $n \geqslant 2$, $r_n$ is divisible by $m^2$ for some integer $m \geqslant 2$;

(b) there exist infinitely many $n \geqslant 2$ for which $r_n=m^2$ for some integer $m \geqslant 2$.

kz.2000.2.7.reviewed_statement:
Is there a function $f : \mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ satisfying the following conditions:

(1) $f(0) = 1$;

(2) $f(x+f(y)) = f(x+y) + 1$ for all $x,y \in\mathbb{R}$;

(3) there exists a rational but non-integer $x_0$ such that $f(x_0)$ is an integer?

kz.2004.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Does there exist a sequence $(a_n)$ of positive integers satisfying the following conditions:

(a) every natural number occurs in the sequence exactly once;

(b) $a_1 + a_2 +\cdots + a_n$ is divisible by $n^n$ for each $n = 1,2,3, \ldots$?

kz.2008.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Find the maximum number of planes in space for which there exist $6$ points satisfying the following conditions:

1. each plane contains at least $4$ of the points;

2. no four of the points are collinear.

cnt.1986.1.2.reviewed_statement:
Let $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n$ and $b_1, b_2, \ldots, b_n$ be $2n$ real numbers. Prove that the following two statements are equivalent:

(i) for any $n$ real numbers $x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n$ satisfying $x_1 \leq x_2 \leq \cdots \leq x_n$ we have $\sum_{k = 1}^{n} a_k x_k \leq \sum_{k = 1}^{n} b_k x_k$;

(ii) $\sum_{k = 1}^{s} a_k \leq \sum_{k = 1}^{s} b_k$ for every $s\in\{1,2,\ldots,n-1\}$, and $\sum_{k = 1}^{n} a_k = \sum_{k = 1}^{n} b_k$.

cnt.1986.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Given a positive integer $A$ with decimal expansion $(a_n,a_{n-1}, \ldots, a_0)$, let $f(A)=\sum_{k=0}^{n} 2^{n-k} a_k$. Define $A_1=f(A)$, $A_2=f(A_1)$, and so on. Prove that

(i) there exists a positive integer $k$ for which $A_{k+1}=A_k$;

(ii) find this $A_k$ for $A=19^{86}$.

cnt.1986.2.6.reviewed_statement:
In a tetrahedron $ABCD$, points $E$, $F$, $G$ lie on the segments $AB$, $AC$, $AD$, respectively. Prove that

(i) $\operatorname{area}(EFG) \leq \max\{\operatorname{area}(ABC),\operatorname{area}(ABD),\operatorname{area}(ACD),\operatorname{area}(BCD)\}$;

(ii) the same holds with "area" replaced by "perimeter".

cnt.1986.2.7.reviewed_statement:
Let $x_1,\ldots,x_n$ be real numbers with $n \geq 3$, and let $p$ and $q$ be their elementary symmetric sums of degree $1$ and $2$, respectively. Prove that

(i) $p^2 \cdot \frac{n-1}{n}-2q \geq 0$;

(ii) $\left|x_i - \frac{p}{n}\right| \leq \frac{n-1}{n}\sqrt{p^2 - \frac{2nq}{n-1}}$ for every $i$.

cnt.1987.1.1.reviewed_statement:
(a) For every positive integer $k$ find the smallest positive integer $f(k)$ such that there exist $5$ sets $s_1,s_2, \ldots , s_5$ satisfying:

(i) each has $k$ elements;

(ii) $s_i$ and $s_{i+1}$ are disjoint for $i=1,2,\ldots,5$ (where $s_6=s_1$);

(iii) the union of the $5$ sets has exactly $f(k)$ elements.

(b) Generalize to $n \geq 3$ sets instead of $5$.

cnt.1988.1.2.reviewed_statement:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{Q} \to \mathbb{C}$ satisfying

(i) $f(x_1 + x_2 + \cdots + x_{1988}) = f(x_1)f(x_2) \cdots f(x_{1988})$ for all $x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_{1988} \in \mathbb{Q}$;

(ii) $\overline{f(1988)}f(x) = f(1988)\overline{f(x)}$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Q}$.

cnt.1989.2.8.reviewed_statement:
For $n \in \mathbb{N}$, $P(n)$ denotes the number of partitions of $n$ into a sum of positive integers (disregarding order); e.g. $P(4)=5$, since $4 = 1+1+1+1 = 1+1+2 = 1+3 = 2+2 = 4$. The dispersion of a partition is the number of distinct parts in it, and $q(n)$ denotes the sum of the dispersions of all partitions of $n$; e.g. $q(4)=1+2+2+1+1=7$. Prove that for $n \geq 1$

(1) $q(n) = 1 + \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} P(i)$;

(2) $1 + \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} P(i) \leq \sqrt{2n}\, P(n)$.

cnt.1990.1.3.reviewed_statement:
On a set $S$ an operation $\circ$ is given which assigns to all $a,b \in S$ an element $a \circ b \in S$, such that

(i) $(a \circ b) \circ c = a \circ (b \circ c)$ for all $a,b,c \in S$;

(ii) $a \circ b \neq b \circ a$ whenever $a \neq b$.

Prove that

(a) $(a \circ b) \circ c = a \circ c$ for all $a,b,c \in S$;

(b) if $S = \{1,2, \ldots, 1990\}$, an operation $\circ$ on $S$ with the above properties can be defined.

cnt.1996.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $M = \{ 2, 3, 4, \ldots, 1000 \}$. Find the smallest $n \in \mathbb{N}$ such that any $n$-element subset of $M$ contains three pairwise disjoint $4$-element subsets $S, T, U$ such that

(I) for any two elements of $S$, the larger is a multiple of the smaller, and the same holds for $T$ and for $U$;

(II) $\gcd(s,t) = 1$ for all $s \in S$, $t \in T$;

(III) $\gcd(s,u) > 1$ for all $s \in S$, $u \in U$.

cnt.1997.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Prove that there exists $m \in \mathbb{N}$ for which there is an integer sequence $(a_n)$ satisfying

(I) $a_0 = 1$, $a_1 = 337$;

(II) $(a_{n + 1} a_{n - 1} - a_n^2) + \frac{3}{4}(a_{n + 1} + a_{n - 1} - 2a_n) = m$ for all $n \geq 1$;

(III) $\frac{1}{6}(a_n + 1)(2a_n + 1)$ is a perfect square for all $n \geq 1$.

cnt.1997.2.4.reviewed_statement:
Find all polynomials $f(x)$ with real coefficients satisfying the following conditions:

(i) $f(x) = a_0 x^{2n} + a_2 x^{2n - 2} + \cdots + a_{2n - 2} x^2 + a_{2n}$, $a_0 > 0$;

(ii) $\sum_{j=0}^n a_{2j} a_{2n - 2j} \leq \binom{2n}{n} a_0 a_{2n}$;

(iii) all roots of $f(x)$ are purely imaginary.

cnt.1998.1.3.reviewed_statement:
For a fixed $\theta \in \left(0, \frac{\pi}{2}\right)$, find the smallest $a \in \mathbb{R}^{+}$ satisfying the following conditions:

(I) $\frac{\sqrt a}{\cos \theta} + \frac{\sqrt a}{\sin \theta} > 1$;

(II) there exists $x \in \left[ 1 - \frac{\sqrt a}{\sin \theta}, \frac{\sqrt a}{\cos \theta} \right]$ such that $$\left[ (1 - x)\sin \theta - \sqrt{a - x^2 \cos^2 \theta} \right]^2 + \left[ x\cos \theta - \sqrt{a - (1 - x)^2 \sin^2 \theta} \right]^2 \leq a.$$

cnt.1999.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $S = \{ 1, 2, \ldots, 15 \}$, and let $A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_n$ be subsets of $S$ satisfying

(I) $|A_i| = 7$ for $i = 1, 2, \ldots, n$;

(II) $|A_i \cap A_j| \leq 3$ for $1 \leq i < j \leq n$;

(III) for any $3$-element subset $M$ of $S$ there exists $A_k$ with $M \subset A_k$.

Find the smallest possible value of $n$.

cnt.1999.2.6.reviewed_statement:
For every permutation $\tau = (x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_{10})$ of $1, 2, \ldots, 10$ define $S(\tau) = \sum_{k = 1}^{10} |2x_k - 3x_{k + 1}|$, where $x_{11} = x_1$. Find

(I) the maximum and the minimum values of $S(\tau)$;

(II) the number of $\tau$ for which $S(\tau)$ attains its maximum;

(III) the number of $\tau$ for which $S(\tau)$ attains its minimum.

cnt.2000.1.3.reviewed_statement:
For an integer $a \geq 2$, let $N_a$ be the number of positive integers $k$ with the following property: the sum of the squares of the digits of $k$ in base $a$ equals $k$. Prove that

(a) $N_a$ is odd;

(b) for every positive integer $M$ there exists an integer $a \geq 2$ with $N_a \geq M$.

cnt.2000.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Let $n$ be a positive integer and $M = \{(x, y)\mid x, y \text{ integers},\ 1 \leq x, y \leq n\}$. Consider functions $f$ on $M$ with the following properties:

(a) $f(x, y)$ takes non-negative integer values;

(b) $\sum_{y=1}^n f(x, y) = n - 1$ for $1 \leq x \leq n$;

(c) if $f(x_1, y_1)f(x_2, y_2) > 0$, then $(x_1 - x_2)(y_1 - y_2) \geq 0$.

Find $N(n)$, the number of functions $f$ satisfying all the conditions, and give the explicit value of $N(4)$.

cnt.2002.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Seventeen football fans planned to go to Korea to watch World Cup matches, and selected $17$ matches. Their bookings of admission tickets satisfied the following conditions:

• each person booked at most one ticket for each match;

• the tickets booked by any two persons had at most one match in common;

• one person booked six tickets.

At most how many tickets did the fans book?

cnt.2006.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Given $n\geq 1$ real numbers $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n$, prove that there exist real numbers $b_1, b_2, \ldots, b_n$ such that

(a) $a_i - b_i$ is a positive integer for every $1 \leq i \leq n$;

(b) $\sum_{1 \leq i < j \leq n} (b_i - b_j)^2 \leq \frac{n^2-1}{12}$.

cnt.2006.8.22.reviewed_statement:
Let $k\geq 3$ be odd. Prove that there exists an integer-valued polynomial $f$ of degree $k$ with non-integer coefficients with the following properties:

(1) $f(0)=0$ and $f(1)=1$;

(2) there exist infinitely many positive integers $n$ such that if the equation $n= f(x_1)+\cdots+f(x_s)$ has a solution in integers $x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_s$, then $s \geq 2^k-1$.

cnt.2008.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Find the maximal constant $M$ such that for every integer $n\geq 3$ there exist two sequences of positive real numbers $a_1,\ldots,a_n$ and $b_1,\ldots,b_n$ satisfying

(1) $\sum_{k = 1}^{n}b_k = 1$ and $2b_k\geq b_{k - 1} + b_{k + 1}$ for $k = 2,3,\ldots,n - 1$;

(2) $a_k^2\leq 1 + \sum_{i = 1}^{k}a_ib_i$ for $k = 1,2,\ldots,n$, and $a_n = M$.

cnt.2010.1.1.reviewed_statement:
Real numbers $a_i,b_i$ ($i=0,1,\ldots,2n$) satisfy the following conditions:

(1) $a_i+a_{i+1}\geq 0$ for $i=0,1,\ldots,2n-1$;

(2) $a_{2j+1}\leq 0$ for $j=0,1,\ldots,n-1$;

(3) $\sum_{k=2p}^{2q}b_k>0$ for all integers $0\leq p\leq q\leq n$.

Prove that $\sum_{i=0}^{2n}(-1)^i a_i b_i\geq 0$, and determine when equality holds.

cnt.2010.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Find all positive integers $m,n\geq 2$ such that

(1) $m+1$ is a prime of the form $4k-1$;

(2) there are a prime $p$ and a nonnegative integer $a$ such that $$\frac{m^{2^n-1}-1}{m-1}=m^n+p^a.$$

cnt.2010.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Let $A$ be a finite set and $A_1,A_2,\ldots, A_n$ subsets of $A$ with the following properties:

(1) $|A_1|=|A_2|=\cdots=|A_n|=k$, where $k>\frac{|A|}{2}$;

(2) for any $a,b\in A$ there exist $A_r,A_s,A_t$ ($1\leq r<s<t\leq n$) with $a,b\in A_r\cap A_s\cap A_t$;

(3) $|A_i\cap A_j|\leq 3$ for all $1\leq i<j\leq n$.

Find all possible values of $n$ when $k$ attains its maximum among all such systems $(A_1,A_2,\ldots, A_n,A)$.

cnt.2010.8.24.reviewed_statement:
Given an integer $n\geq 2$ and real numbers $x_1,x_2,\ldots, x_n$ in $[0,1]$, prove that there exist real numbers $a_0,a_1,\ldots,a_n$ satisfying

(1) $a_0+a_n=0$;

(2) $|a_i|\leq 1$ for $i=0,1,\ldots,n$;

(3) $|a_i-a_{i-1}|=x_i$ for $i=1,2,\ldots,n$.

cnt.2012.4.10.reviewed_statement:
Given an integer $n\ge 2$, prove that there exist only finitely many $n$-tuples of positive integers $(a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n)$ which simultaneously satisfy the following three conditions:

• $a_1>a_2>\ldots>a_n$;

• $\gcd (a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n)=1$;

• $a_1=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\gcd (a_i,a_{i+1})$, where $a_{n+1}=a_1$.

cnt.2013.1.3.reviewed_statement:
There are $n$ balls numbered $1,2,\ldots,n$. They are painted with $4$ colours, red, yellow, blue and green, according to the following rules. First, place them on a circle in an arbitrary order. Then, for any three clockwise consecutive balls numbered $i, j, k$ in this order:

1) if $i>j>k$, the ball $j$ is painted red;

2) if $i<j<k$, the ball $j$ is painted yellow;

3) if $i<j$ and $k<j$, the ball $j$ is painted blue;

4) if $i>j$ and $k>j$, the ball $j$ is painted green.

Thus each arrangement of the balls determines a painting. Two paintings are called distinct if some ball receives different colours in them. Find the number of distinct paintings.

cnt.2013.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Find all positive real numbers $r<1$ such that there exists a set $\mathcal{S}$ with the following properties:

i) for any real number $t$, exactly one of $t, t+r$ and $t+1$ belongs to $\mathcal{S}$;

ii) for any real number $t$, exactly one of $t, t-r$ and $t-1$ belongs to $\mathcal{S}$.

cnt.2013.3.8.reviewed_statement:
Prove that there exist a positive constant $K$ and a sequence of integers $(a_n)$ satisfying:

(1) $0<a_1<a_2<\cdots <a_n<\cdots$;

(2) for any positive integer $n$, $a_n<1.01^n K$;

(3) the sum of any finite number of distinct terms of $(a_n)$ is not a perfect square.

cnt.2017.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $S=\{1,2,3,\ldots,2017\}$. For every subset $A$ of $S$ a real number $f(A)\geq 0$ is defined such that:

(1) for any $A,B\subseteq S$, $f(A\cup B)+f(A\cap B)\leq f(A)+f(B)$;

(2) for any $A\subseteq B\subseteq S$, $f(A)\leq f(B)$;

(3) for any $k,j\in S$, $$f(\{1,2,\ldots,k+1\})\geq f(\{1,2,\ldots,k\}\cup \{j\});$$

(4) $f(\varnothing)=0$.

Prove that for any three-element subset $T$ of $S$, the inequality $$f(T)\leq \frac{27}{19}f(\{1,2,3\})$$ holds.

cnt.2017.4.12.reviewed_statement:
Let $M$ be a subset of $\mathbb{R}$ such that the following conditions are satisfied:

a) for any $x \in M$ and $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, one has $x+n \in M$;

b) for any $x \in M$, one has $-x \in M$;

c) both $M$ and $\mathbb{R}\setminus M$ contain an interval of positive length.

For any real $x$, let $M(x) = \{ n \in \mathbb{Z}^{+} \mid nx \in M \}$. Show that if $\alpha,\beta$ are reals such that $M(\alpha) = M(\beta)$, then one of $\alpha + \beta$ and $\alpha - \beta$ is rational.

cnt.2017.7.21.reviewed_statement:
Find the number of ordered $100$-tuples $(x_1,\ldots,x_{100})$ satisfying the following conditions:

(i) $x_1,\ldots,x_{100}\in\{1,2,\ldots,2017\}$;

(ii) $2017\mid x_1+\cdots+x_{100}$;

(iii) $2017\mid x_1^2+\cdots+x_{100}^2$.

cnt.2017.10.30.reviewed_statement:
A graph with $n$ vertices is called $k$-flowing-chromatic if:

1. one can place a chess piece, coloured with one of $k$ colours, on each vertex so that any two pieces on adjacent vertices have different colours;

2. one can choose a Hamiltonian cycle $v_1,v_2,\ldots , v_n$ such that, after moving the piece on $v_i$ to $v_{i+1}$ for all $i=1,2,\ldots ,n$ (with $v_{n+1}=v_1$), any two pieces on adjacent vertices still have different colours;

3. after performing step 2 some number of times, every piece has visited each of the $n$ vertices.

Let $T(G)$ denote the least number $k$ such that $G$ is $k$-flowing-chromatic; if no such $k$ exists, let $T(G)=0$. Let $\chi (G)$ denote the chromatic number of $G$. Find all positive integers $m$ for which there is a graph $G$ with $\chi (G)\le m$ and $T(G)\ge 2^m$ that has no cycle of length less than $2017$.

cnt.2018.4.12.reviewed_statement:
Let $M,a,b,r$ be non-negative integers with $a,r\ge 2$, and suppose there exists a function $f:\mathbb{Z}\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}$ satisfying the following conditions:

(1) for all $n\in \mathbb{Z}$, $f^{(r)}(n)=an+b$, where $f^{(r)}$ denotes the composition of $r$ copies of $f$;

(2) for all $n\ge M$, $f(n)\ge 0$;

(3) for all $n>m>M$, $n-m\mid f(n)-f(m)$.

Show that $a$ is a perfect $r$-th power.

cnt.2019.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Find all positive integers $n$ such that there exist $n$ points $P_1,\ldots,P_n$ on the unit circle with the property that $\sum_{i=1}^n MP_i^k$ takes the same value for every point $M$ on the unit circle, where

a) $k=2018$;

b) $k=2019$.

cnt.2019.6.16.reviewed_statement:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R}^2 \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ such that

1) $f(0,x)$ is non-decreasing in $x$;

2) for any $x,y \in \mathbb{R}$, $f(x,y)=f(y,x)$;

3) for any $x,y,z \in \mathbb{R}$, $(f(x,y)-f(y,z))(f(y,z)-f(z,x))(f(z,x)-f(x,y))=0$;

4) for any $x,y,a \in \mathbb{R}$, $f(x+a,y+a)=f(x,y)+a$.

cnt.2021.7.19.reviewed_statement:
Let $n\ge2$ be a positive integer. Find the minimum $m$ such that there exist $x_{ij}$ ($1\le i ,j\le n$) satisfying:

(1) for every $1\le i ,j\le n$, $x_{ij}=\max\{x_{i1},x_{i2},\ldots,x_{ij}\}$ or $x_{ij}=\max\{x_{1j},x_{2j},\ldots,x_{ij}\}$;

(2) for every $1\le i \le n$, there are at most $m$ indices $k$ with $x_{ik}=\max\{x_{i1},x_{i2},\ldots,x_{ik}\}$;

(3) for every $1\le j \le n$, there are at most $m$ indices $k$ with $x_{kj}=\max\{x_{1j},x_{2j},\ldots,x_{kj}\}$.

cnt.2022.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $a, b, c, p, q, r$ be positive integers with $p, q, r \ge 2$. Denote $$Q=\{(x, y, z)\in \mathbb{Z}^3 : 0 \le x \le a,\ 0 \le y \le b ,\ 0 \le z \le c \}.$$ Initially, some pieces are placed on the points of $Q$, $M$ pieces in total. Then one can repeatedly perform the following three types of operations:

(1) remove $p$ pieces from $(x, y, z)$ and place a piece on $(x-1, y, z)$;

(2) remove $q$ pieces from $(x, y, z)$ and place a piece on $(x, y-1, z)$;

(3) remove $r$ pieces from $(x, y, z)$ and place a piece on $(x, y, z-1)$.

Find the smallest positive integer $M$ such that, no matter how the pieces are distributed initially, one can always perform a sequence of operations that places a piece on $(0,0,0)$.

cnt.2022.5.15.reviewed_statement:
Given a positive integer $n \ge 2$, find all $n$-tuples of positive integers $(a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n)$ such that $1<a_1 \le a_2 \le a_3 \le \cdots \le a_n$, $a_1$ is odd, and

(1) $M=\frac{1}{2^n}(a_1-1)a_2 a_3 \cdots a_n$ is a positive integer;

(2) one can pick $n$-tuples of integers $(k_{i,1},k_{i,2},\ldots,k_{i,n})$ for $i=1,2,\ldots,M$ such that for any $1 \le i_1 <i_2 \le M$ there exists $j \in \{1,2,\ldots,n\}$ with $k_{i_1,j}-k_{i_2,j} \not\equiv 0, \pm 1 \pmod{a_j}$.

cnt.2022.7.19.reviewed_statement:
Initially, each unit square of an $n \times n$ grid is coloured red, yellow or blue. In each round, the following operation is performed for every unit square simultaneously:

• a red square that has a common edge with a yellow square is coloured yellow;

• a yellow square that has a common edge with a blue square is coloured blue;

• a blue square that has a common edge with a red square is coloured red.

It is known that after several rounds all unit squares of the grid have the same colour. Prove that the grid became monochromatic no later than the end of the $(2n-2)$-th round.

cnt.2023.3.8.reviewed_statement:
In a non-isosceles acute triangle $ABC$ with orthocenter $H$, let $AP$, $BQ$, $CR$ be the altitudes. Let $A_1$ be the midpoint of $BC$; $AA_1$ intersects $QR$ at $K$, and $QR$ intersects the line through $A$ parallel to $BC$ at $D$. The line connecting $K$ and the midpoint of $AH$ intersects $DA_1$ at $A_2$. Define $B_2$, $C_2$ similarly. Triangle $A_2B_2C_2$ is known to be non-degenerate; let $\omega$ be its circumcircle. Prove that there are circles $\odot A'$, $\odot B'$, $\odot C'$, internally tangent to $\omega$, such that:

(1) $\odot A'$ is tangent to $AB$ and $AC$, $\odot B'$ is tangent to $BC$ and $BA$, and $\odot C'$ is tangent to $CA$ and $CB$;

(2) $A'$, $B'$, $C'$ are distinct and collinear.

cnt.2023.3.9.reviewed_statement:
Find the largest positive integer $m$ for which it is possible to colour some cells of a $70\times 70$ table red such that

• there are no two red cells such that the two rows containing them have the same number of red cells and the two columns containing them also have the same number of red cells;

• there are two rows with exactly $m$ red cells each.

cnt.2023.4.12.reviewed_statement:
Prove that there exists a positive real number $\lambda$ such that for any real number $D>1$ one can find an acute triangle $ABC$ in the Cartesian plane such that

• $A, B, C$ are lattice points;

• $AB, BC, CA>D$;

• the area of triangle $ABC$ is less than $\frac{\sqrt 3}{4}D^2+\lambda\cdot D^{4/5}$.

cnt.2023.6.17.reviewed_statement:
Do there exist pairwise distinct integers $a_1, a_2, \ldots$ satisfying:

(1) $a_{k^2}>0$ and $a_{k^2+k}<0$ for all positive integers $k$;

(2) $\left| a_{n+1}-a_n\right|\leq 2023\sqrt n$ for all positive integers $n$?

cnt.2025.4.12.reviewed_statement:
Let $P(x), Q(x)$ be non-constant real polynomials such that for every positive integer $m$ there exists a positive integer $n$ with $P(m) = Q(n)$. Prove that:

(1) if $\deg Q \mid \deg P$, then there exists a real polynomial $h(x)$ such that $P(x) = Q(h(x))$ for all real numbers $x$;

(2) $\deg Q \mid \deg P$.

cnt.2025.6.17.reviewed_statement:
Prove that there exist integers $x_1,x_2,\ldots, x_{10},y_1,y_2,\ldots, y_{10}$ satisfying the following conditions:

(1) $|x_i|,|y_i|\le 10^{10}$ for all $1\le i \le 10$;

(2) the set $$S = \left\{ \left( \sum_{i=1}^{10} a_i x_i, \sum_{i=1}^{10} a_i y_i \right) : a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_{10} \in \{0, 1\} \right\}$$ has $|S| = 1024$, and any strip of width $1$ (the region between two parallel lines at distance $1$) covers at most two points of $S$.

vnt.1985.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Does there exist a triangle $ABC$ satisfying the following two conditions:

(a) $\sin^2A + \sin^2B + \sin^2C = \cot A + \cot B + \cot C$;

(b) $S\ge a^2 - (b - c)^2$, where $S$ is the area of triangle $ABC$?

vnt.1994.1.1.reviewed_statement:
Given a parallelogram $ABCD$, let $E$ be a point on the side $BC$ and $F$ a point on the side $CD$ such that the triangles $ABE$ and $BCF$ have the same area. The diagonal $BD$ meets $AE$ at $M$ and $AF$ at $N$. Prove that:

I. there exists a triangle whose sides are equal to $BM, MN, ND$;

II. when $E, F$ vary so that the length of $MN$ decreases, the circumradius of this triangle also decreases.

vnt.1997.1.2.reviewed_statement:
There are $25$ towns in a country. Find the smallest $k$ for which one can set up two-way flight routes between these towns so that the following conditions are satisfied:

1) from each town there are direct routes to exactly $k$ other towns;

2) if two towns are not connected by a direct route, then there is a town which has direct routes to both of them.

vnt.1997.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Find the greatest real number $\alpha$ for which there exists an infinite sequence of integers $(a_n)_{n\ge1}$ satisfying the following conditions:

1) $a_n > 1997n$ for every positive integer $n$;

2) for every $n\ge 2$, $U_n\ge a_n^{\alpha}$, where $U_n = \gcd\{a_i + a_k \mid i + k = n\}$.

vnt.1998.2.6.reviewed_statement:
At a conference there are $n \geq 10$ people. It is known that:

I. each person knows at least $\left\lfloor\frac{n+2}{3}\right\rfloor$ other people;

II. for any two people $A$ and $B$ who do not know each other, there exist people $A(1), A(2), \ldots, A(k)$ such that $A$ knows $A(1)$, $A(i)$ knows $A(i+1)$ for each $i$, and $A(k)$ knows $B$;

III. there is no Hamiltonian path.

Prove that the people can be divided into two groups such that one group has a Hamiltonian cycle and the other consists of people no two of whom know each other.

vnt.1999.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $H$ be a convex polygon. Show that for every real number $a \in (0, 1)$ there exist $6$ distinct points $A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_6$ on the sides of $H$, in clockwise order, satisfying the conditions:

I. $\overrightarrow{A_1A_2} = \overrightarrow{A_5A_4} = a \cdot \overrightarrow{A_6A_3}$;

II. the lines $A_1A_2$ and $A_5A_4$ are equidistant from $A_6A_3$.

vnt.2002.1.2.reviewed_statement:
A positive integer $n_0$ is written on a blackboard. Two players $A$ and $B$ play the following game:

• moving alternately, each player deletes the number $n_k$ written on the blackboard and writes instead a number $n_{k+1}$ from the set $\left\{n_k-1, \left\lfloor\frac {n_k}3\right\rfloor\right\}$;

• player $A$ moves first, replacing $n_0$ with $n_1\in\left\{n_0-1, \left\lfloor\frac {n_0}3\right\rfloor\right\}$;

• the game ends when the number on the board is $0$, and the player who wrote it wins.

Find which player has a winning strategy in each of the following cases: a) $n_0=120$; b) $n_0=\frac {3^{2002}-1}2$; c) $n_0=\frac{3^{2002}+1}2$.

vnt.2002.1.3.reviewed_statement:
Let $m$ be a positive integer which has a prime divisor greater than $\sqrt {2m} +1$. Find the minimal positive integer $n$ for which there exists a finite set $S$ of distinct positive integers satisfying the following two conditions:

I. $m\leq x\leq n$ for all $x\in S$;

II. the product of all elements of $S$ is a perfect square.

vnt.2004.2.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $(x_n)_{n\ge1}$ be the sequence defined by $x_1 = 603$, $x_2 = 102$ and $$x_{n + 2} = x_{n + 1} + x_n + 2\sqrt {x_{n + 1} x_n - 2}\qquad (n \geq 1).$$ Show that:

(1) $x_n$ is a positive integer for every $n \geq 1$;

(2) there are infinitely many positive integers $n$ for which the decimal representation of $x_n$ ends with $2003$;

(3) there is no positive integer $n$ for which the decimal representation of $x_n$ ends with $2004$.

vnt.2005.2.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $p>3$ be a prime. Calculate:

a) $S=\sum_{k=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} \left(\left\lfloor\frac{2k^2}{p}\right\rfloor-2 \left\lfloor\frac{k^2}{p}\right\rfloor\right)$ if $p\equiv 1 \pmod 4$;

b) $T=\sum_{k=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} \left\lfloor\frac{k^2}{p}\right\rfloor$ if $p\equiv 1 \pmod 8$.

vnt.2007.1.1.reviewed_statement:
Let $A, B$ be two sets of positive real numbers such that $|A| = |B| =n$, $A \neq B$ and $S(A)=S(B)$, where $|X|$ is the number of elements and $S(X)$ is the sum of all elements of a set $X$. Prove that one can fill the unit squares of an $n\times n$ board with positive numbers and zeros such that:

a) the set of the row sums equals $A$;

b) the set of the column sums equals $B$;

c) there are at least $(n-1)^{2}+k$ zeros in the board, where $k=|A \cap B|$.

vnt.2012.1.2.reviewed_statement:
Consider an $m\times n$ rectangular grid with $m$ rows and $n$ columns. There are water fountains on some of the squares. A fountain can spray water onto any of its adjacent squares, or onto a square in the same column such that there is exactly one square between them. Find the minimum number of fountains such that every square can be sprayed, in the cases

a) $m=4$;

b) $m=3$.

vnt.2015.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Find the smallest positive integer $n$ for which there exist $n$ real numbers $a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n$ satisfying the three conditions

a. $a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_n>0$;

b. $a_1^3+a_2^3+\cdots+a_n^3<0$;

c. $a_1^5+a_2^5+\cdots+a_n^5>0$.

vnt.2016.2.6.reviewed_statement:
Given $16$ distinct real numbers $\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\ldots,\alpha_{16}$, for each polynomial $P$ let $$V(P)=P(\alpha_1)+P(\alpha_2)+\cdots+P(\alpha_{16}).$$ Prove that there is a monic polynomial $Q$ of degree $8$ such that:

i) $V(QP)=0$ for every polynomial $P$ with $\deg P<8$;

ii) $Q$ has $8$ real roots (counted with multiplicity).

vnt.2023.1.2.reviewed_statement:
Consider the three functions $$P(x) = (x^2-1)^{2023},\qquad Q(x) = (2x+1)^{14},\qquad R(x) = \left(2x+1+\frac 2x \right)^{34}.$$ Initially, we pick a set $S$ containing two of these functions, and we perform operations on it. The allowed operations are:

• take two functions $p,q \in S$ and add one of $p+q$, $p-q$, $pq$ to $S$;

• take a function $p \in S$ and add $p^k$ to $S$, for any positive integer $k$ of our choice;

• take a function $p \in S$ and a real number $t$, and add one of the functions $p+t$, $p-t$, $pt$ to $S$.

Show that no matter which two functions are picked initially, no finite sequence of operations on $S$ yields the third function.

vnt.2023.2.4.reviewed_statement:
Let $a, b$ be coprime positive integers with $b$ odd and $a>2$. The sequence $(x_n)$ is defined by $x_0=2$, $x_1=a$ and $x_{n+2}=ax_{n+1}+bx_n$ for $n \geq 0$. Prove that:

a) if $a$ is even, then there do not exist positive integers $m, n, p$ such that $\frac{x_m} {x_nx_p}$ is a positive integer;

b) if $a$ is odd, then there do not exist positive integers $m, n, p$ such that $mnp$ is even and $\frac{x_m} {x_nx_p}$ is a perfect square.

vnt.2024.1.2.reviewed_statement:
A garden is organized as a $2024\times 2024$ board, and three types of flowers are planted in it: roses, daisies and orchids. The planting must satisfy the following conditions:

(i) each cell is planted with at most one type of flower; some cells may be left empty;

(ii) for each planted cell $A$, there are exactly $3$ other planted cells in the same row or column as $A$ that are planted with flowers of types different from that of $A$;

(iii) each type of flower is planted in at least $1$ cell.

What is the maximal number of cells that can be planted?

int.2001.3.7.reviewed_statement:
On the sides of a triangle $ABC$, triangles $AEB$ and $AFC$ are constructed externally such that $\angle AEB=2 \alpha$, $\angle AFC= 2 \beta$, $AE=EB$ and $AF=FC$. On $BC$, triangle $BDC$ is constructed externally with $\angle DBC= \beta$ and $\angle BCD= \alpha$. Prove that:

1. $DA$ is perpendicular to $EF$;

2. if $T$ is the projection of $D$ on $BC$, then $\frac{DA}{EF}= 2 \frac{DT}{BC}$.

int.2005.1.2.reviewed_statement:
Prove that there exists $n_{0} \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for every $m \geq n_{0}$ there exist three positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$ such that

(i) $m^3 < a < b < c < (m+1)^3$;

(ii) $abc$ is the cube of an integer.

int.2005.2.6.reviewed_statement:
There are $10001$ students at a university. Some students join together to form several clubs (a student may belong to different clubs), and some clubs join together to form several societies (a club may belong to different societies). There are $k$ societies in total. Suppose that the following conditions hold:

i) each pair of students are in exactly one common club;

ii) for each student and each society, the student is in exactly one club of the society;

iii) each club has an odd number of students, and a club with $2m+1$ students ($m$ a positive integer) is in exactly $m$ societies.

Find all possible values of $k$.

int.2006.2.4.reviewed_statement:
